\subsection{Core Result: Involutive Automorphism Is Sufficient but Confined to Same-Orbit Pairs}

\subsubsection{Forward Direction}

Let $\phi$ be a non-trivial involution, $\phi(e_1)=e_2$. Let $H_1(s)$ and $H_2(s)$ be the Hamiltonians with $e_1$ and $e_2$ as defect edges. Since $\phi$ is a graph automorphism and $\phi(e_1)=e_2$,
\begin{equation}
H_2(s) = U_\phi H_1(s) U_\phi^\dagger,
\end{equation}
where $U_\phi$ is the permutation operator. The ground-state density matrix satisfies $\rho_2 = U_\phi \rho_1 U_\phi^\dagger$. For any edge $f$, the sorted correlation distribution is invariant under permutation. Hence $\mathcal{R}_{e_2}(s) = \mathcal{R}_{e_1}(s)$. This proves the forward direction.

Numerically, all involution images in all 112 graphs with $N=6$ and all 853 graphs with $N=7$ have the same response.

\subsubsection{Reverse Direction Does Not Hold}

On $N=6$ and $N=7$, all involution images lie within the same orbit. None of the different-orbit same-response pairs is an involution image.

