\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Relationship with Standard Physics}

The result ``same orbit implies same response'' is a direct consequence of graph automorphism symmetry. This work verifies it with zero counterexamples on all connected graphs with $N=6$ and $N=7$, for all four interaction rules, and delineates its boundary.

The mechanism is resolved as a layered structure. Layer 1 ($S_1 = S_2$) is universal. Layer 2 ($|sd_1| = |sd_2|$ at $s=1/2$) holds for both $S$ branches. Layer 3 (sign rule) is determined by the total spin. The sufficient condition is full $p_S(s)$-curve equality. The singlet probability admits the exact analytic form $p_S(s) = (1 - dE_0/ds)/4$ and is monotonically non-decreasing. Equal-value windows terminate exactly at $s=0$ or $s=1$. This layered picture explains why no single graph-theoretic condition could discriminate the core layer: the mechanism is quantum, not graph-theoretic.

\subsection{Relationship with Relational Ontology}

Relational ontology states that the properties of an object are determined by its position in the relational network. Response equivalence is a concrete realization. The involution is a global symmetry of the relational field.

The different-orbit same-response pairs show that the relational position is not fully captured by the automorphism orbit. The mechanism is a layered quantum resonance: total spin equality, absolute coupling equality, sign rule, and $p_S$-curve equality.

\section{Limitations and Open Problems}

\begin{enumerate}
\item Finite size: exhaustive verification for $N=6$ and $N=7$. $N=8$ requires full enumeration.
\item Layered mechanism beyond Layer 3: Layers 1, 2, 3 are necessary with zero false negatives. The sufficient condition (full $p_S$-curve equality) covers 29 of 47 pairs. The remaining 18 pairs require a weaker sufficient condition.
\item Core layer: no graph-theoretic quantity discriminates the 24 core pairs. The core and middle layers are distinguished by the $p_S$-curve height.
\item Degenerate ground states: for integer-spin ($N=6$) and half-integer-spin ($N=7$) degeneracies the $-1$ hit rate differs. A complete mechanism remains open.
\item Model dependence: Ising has 493 different-orbit same-response pairs, likely degeneracy artifacts.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

For all 112 connected graphs with $N=6$ and all 853 connected graphs with $N=7$ in the quantum Heisenberg model:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Same orbit implies same response: zero counterexamples for $N=6$ and $N=7$, and for all four interaction rules.
\item Different orbits can give the same response: 47 pairs for $N=6$, 382 pairs for $N=7$.
\item The mechanism for the 47 pairs is a layered structure. Layer 1: $S_1 = S_2$ (47/47 recall). Layer 2: $|sd_1| = |sd_2|$ at $s = 1/2$ (47/47 recall). Layer 3: sign rule determined by $S$ (47/47 recall). The sufficient condition is full $p_S(s)$-curve equality (29/29 precision, 29/47 recall).
\item The singlet probability admits the exact analytic form $p_S(s) = (1 - dE_0/ds)/4$ and is monotonically non-decreasing. Equal-value windows terminate exactly at the physical boundaries $s=0$ and $s=1$.
\item For $S=1/2$ states, $p_S = 1/2 \Leftrightarrow \langle\boldsymbol{\sigma}\cdot\boldsymbol{\sigma}\rangle = -1$ holds exactly by the Wigner-Eckart theorem.
\item For the core layer of 24 pairs, no graph-theoretic quantity discriminates it. The core and middle layers are distinguished by the $p_S$-curve height.
\item For degenerate ground states, the mechanism remains open.
\end{enumerate}
The boundary of the data is drawn. Same orbit implies same response. Different orbits may also give the same response, through a layered quantum resonance mechanism with analytic $p_S(s)$ and physical boundaries at $s=0$ and $s=1$.

